claims.text:
🟡 **Medium**

**Broad catch in isCliEntrypoint hides unexpected filesystem errors**

This `catch {}` block is empty and catches all errors indiscriminately. An `ENOENT` (the symlink target was removed mid-process) is expected and safe to silently return `false` for. An `EPERM` or `EIO` on an otherwise valid path is not — the CLI will silently not start with no message to the user. At minimum, errors other than `ENOENT` should be logged before returning `false`, keeping the silent-failure fix this PR introduces consistent.

*Suggested fix:*

```suggestion
}	catch (err: unknown) {
		// ENOENT is expected when the symlink target is gone; anything else is
		// a genuine filesystem problem that warrants a log so the user isn't
		// left with a CLI that silently refuses to run.
		if (err instanceof Error && (err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== 'ENOENT') {
			console.error('[cli] isCliEntrypoint: unexpected error resolving path', err);
		}
		return false;
	}
```

🟡 **Medium**

**Test asserts specific mock.calls index without guarding against empty calls array**

The assertion `vi.mocked(console.error).mock.calls[0] as [string]` will silently produce `undefined` (and the subsequent `expect(message).toContain(...)` will pass vacuously with a `contains` on `undefined`) if `cmdAudit` happens to call `console.error` zero times or in a different order. This makes the test self-defeating: the exact failure mode it was added to catch (no error message surfaced) would not be detected.

Use `toHaveBeenCalledWith` on the spy directly, or assert `mock.calls.length > 0` before indexing.

*Suggested fix:*

```suggestion
		const calls = vi.mocked(console.error).mock.calls;
		expect(calls.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
		const [message] = calls[0] as [string];
		expect(message).toContain("GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY is not set");
		expect(message).not.toContain("[object Object]");
```

🟢 **Low**

**Test assertion on console.error call index is fragile**

The test does `vi.mocked(console.error).mock.calls[0]` to get the first call. If any code path (e.g., module-level side effects or other mocks) triggers `console.error` before the `cmdAudit` call under test, this index will be wrong and the assertion will silently pass against the wrong message or throw with a confusing error. Use `expect(errorSpy).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.stringContaining("GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY is not set"))` instead to make the assertion order-independent.

*Suggested fix:*

```suggestion
        expect(vi.mocked(console.error)).toHaveBeenCalledWith(
            expect.stringContaining("GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY is not set"),
        );
        expect(vi.mocked(console.error)).not.toHaveBeenCalledWith(
            expect.stringContaining("[object Object]"),
        );
```
